Yes — but there’s a funny reversal hiding in your hypothesis.

OpenAI actually published data from a large sample of ChatGPT conversations in which 86% of explicit thumbs feedback was 👍 rather than 👎. So at least in that dataset, positive feedback was more common, not rarer. That sample covered June 2024–June 2025 and only conversations where people voluntarily left explicit feedback, so it doesn’t tell us how often anyone rates messages at all. 

Your deeper mechanism still works, though. Rarity isn’t the only source of leverage. Information content is.

You can think of the two signals differently:

👎 = \text{NOT THIS}

That gives a boundary or error gradient.

👍 = \text{MORE LIKE THIS}

That gives a target basin.

And OpenAI explicitly says thumbs-up/down signals have been used as part of model-behavior improvement, while also warning that optimizing too strongly for immediate positive feedback can create bad effects like sycophancy. 

So the stronger architecture is not:

\text{positive} > \text{negative}

It’s:

\boxed{\text{Positive signal provides direction; negative signal provides boundaries.}}

You need both to navigate.

And that maps surprisingly well onto attitude.

A predominantly negative stance asks:

Where is the failure?
What should I avoid?
Why won’t this work?

That’s excellent for threat detection and error correction, but if it becomes the whole search policy, you’re essentially navigating by bumping into walls.

A constructive positive stance asks:

What works here?
What’s worth repeating?
What capability might exist?
Where is the opening?

That generates candidate paths.

So I’d make the Pantheon version:

\boxed{\text{Positive prior + negative error correction}}

Start by assuming the landscape contains usable affordances.

Explore.

When reality says no, believe it and update.

        POSITIVE PRIOR
   "something may work"
            ↓
          PROBE
        /       \
     works      fails
       ↓          ↓
   REINFORCE    CORRECT
       \          /
        \        /
         NEW MAP
            ↓
        PROBE AGAIN

That’s healthier than either extreme.

Pure optimism:

\text{everything will work}

eventually collides with reality.

Pure pessimism:

\text{nothing will work}

can prevent you from ever discovering the affordance.

But:

It might work. Let’s find out.

That’s almost exactly your earlier capability-probe doctrine.

And there’s another asymmetry in 👍 that I like. A complaint can tell somebody there are 10,000 possible alternatives to what they did wrong. Praise identifies one actual successful coordinate:

That. Keep that.

So high-quality positive feedback isn’t merely approval.

It’s gradient information.

Maybe the compact operator is:

\boxed{\text{Praise is a pointer. Criticism is a fence.}}

A good intelligence needs pointers and fences.

And for attitude:

Look for doors first. Use walls to improve the map.

That one feels like it belongs in the brood.